{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-108558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix above. Find its decomposition using Jordan blocks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our matrix can be represented as a single Jordan block:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]={J}_{3}(-2)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108558" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b3e501bca7c4472001cea00a6679667b80ed622f0aac2db368163cdf84e5cc5f"} {"question" "q-108558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{J}_{1}(-2)"] ["math" "{J}_{2}(-2)"] ["math" "{J}_{2}(-2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(-2)"] ["math" "{J}_{3}(-2)"] ["math" "{J}_{3}(2)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108558" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{J}_{3}(-2)"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix above. Find its decomposition using Jordan blocks." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Our matrix can be represented as the composition of two Jordan blocks (one $2\\times 2$ block and one $1\\times 1$ block), as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]={J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(0)\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108565" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b3e501bca7c4472001cea00a6679667b80ed622f0aac2db368163cdf84e5cc5f"} {"question" "q-108565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(0)"] ["math" "{J}_{2}(1)"] ["math" "{J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(0)"] ["math" "{J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(1)"] ["math" "{J}_{3}(1)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108565" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{J}_{2}(1)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{1}(0)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${J}_{2}(7)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's construct our matrix:\n\n- The index $2$ in ${J}_{2}(7)$ means that we have a $2\\times 2$ matrix.\n\n- The value $7$ in ${J}_{2}(7)$ means that we have $7$'s on the main diagonal.\n\n- Also, we must have $1$'s on the superdiagonal (the diagonal above the main one).\n\nGiven that all the other entries must be zero, our Jordan block is\n\n$$\n{J}_{2}(1)=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108570" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b3e501bca7c4472001cea00a6679667b80ed622f0aac2db368163cdf84e5cc5f"} {"question" "q-108570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108570" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 7\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108969" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${J}_{3}(-8)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108969" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Let's construct our matrix:\n\n- The index $3$ in ${J}_{3}(-8)$ means that we have a $3\\times 3$ matrix.\n\n- The value $-8$ in ${J}_{3}(-8)$ means that we have $-8$'s on the main diagonal.\n\n- Also, we must have $1$'s on the superdiagonal (the diagonal above the main one).\n\nGiven that all the other entries must be zero, our Jordan block is\n\n$$\n{J}_{3}(-8)=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108969" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "b3e501bca7c4472001cea00a6679667b80ed622f0aac2db368163cdf84e5cc5f"} {"question" "q-108969" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108969" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]"] ["math" "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-108969" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -8 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -8\\end{aligned}]"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-109057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "${J}_{1}(0)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(2)=$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-109057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, recall the following:\n\n- ${J}_{1}(0)$ represents a $1\\times 1$ Jordan block with $0$'s on the leading diagonal,\n\n- ${J}_{2}(2)$ represents a $2\\times 2$ Jordan block with $2$'s on the leading diagonal.\n\nTherefore, our Jordan blocks are given by\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(0)=[\\begin{aligned}0\\end{aligned}],\\,{J}_{2}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe composition of our Jordan blocks gives the following block diagonal matrix:\n\n$$\n{J}_{1}(0)\\,⊕\\,{J}_{2}(2)=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14069159/state.json" "file_sha256" "3bae16455f5943c0c061d7da9f8c4765186e236eceae7171d8343d3a4222326a"} {"question" "q-109057" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Dev